0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","1.5",{"topic":{"id":"1.5","title":"Types of Internet Crimes and Cyber Offences","minutes":15,"summary":"Recognize identity theft, intrusion, social-media abuse, deepfakes, digital arrest scams, terrorism, and espionage without confusing their legal definitions.","sections":[{"title":"Computer as target and computer as instrument","body":["A computer-target offence attacks the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of a system: unauthorized access, damaging malware, or denial of service. A computer-enabled offence uses technology to facilitate fraud, stalking, extortion, impersonation, or unlawful trade. Many incidents combine both, such as mailbox compromise followed by invoice diversion. The classification should guide evidence collection rather than become a rigid label.","Identity theft under IT Act section 66C concerns fraudulent or dishonest use of specified identification features. Cheating by personation under section 66D focuses on inducing deception through an assumed identity. General cheating under BNS section 318 may also be relevant. A stolen password, a fake profile, and a transferred payment can be different stages of one incident, each requiring proof."]},{"title":"Social harm and emerging deception","body":["Cyberstalking, voyeurism, obscene or sexually explicit electronic material, impersonation, and harassment involve different offences and victims’ rights. Preserve URLs, account identifiers, messages, context, and timestamps sensitively. Do not redistribute intimate material as part of an investigation. Deepfakes may facilitate fraud, defamation, impersonation, or sexual abuse; “deepfake” is not itself a complete statement of the charge.","A digital arrest scam involves a caller falsely claiming that police, a court, or another authority has confined or will arrest the victim unless money is paid. “Digital arrest” is not a recognized lawful arrest procedure or a standalone offence by that name. Investigate personation, cheating, extortion, or wrongful confinement as supported by facts. Genuine agencies do not demand transfers to a “safe account” to avoid arrest."]},{"title":"Terrorism, espionage, and ordinary fraud","body":["Cyber terrorism is narrower than any serious computer incident. IT Act section 66F addresses specified acts intended to threaten India’s unity, integrity, security, or sovereignty, or strike terror, including conduct affecting critical information infrastructure under its conditions. BNS section 113 also has a distinct definition and safeguards. Ransomware is not automatically terrorism.","Cyber espionage describes covert acquisition of sensitive information, often for a state or organization. Attribution requires technical evidence plus context; an IP address or language setting is not conclusive. Classify the observed conduct, the victim’s harm, the suspected motive, and the legal basis separately. Use neutral terms until attribution is sufficiently supported."]}],"practice":{"title":"The video-call “police officer”","scenario":"A caller in uniform shows a forged warrant and tells a student to transfer money to avoid immediate arrest.","steps":["Stop payment and preserve the number, transaction reference, screenshots, and call details.","Identify the false identity, threatened harm, and induced transfer.","Discuss personation, cheating, and extortion without assuming terrorism.","Contact the bank and the official cybercrime channel; verify agency contact independently."],"debrief":"The label “digital arrest” describes the scam narrative. The legal analysis still depends on conduct and statutory ingredients."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bns"],"sources":["itact","bns","cybercrime","i4c","deepfake"],"labId":"email","unit":1,"objectives":["Explain computer as target and computer as instrument and apply it to an investigation.","Explain social harm and emerging deception and apply it to an investigation.","Explain terrorism, espionage, and ordinary fraud and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.5","prompt":"What should you do if a caller demands a transfer to avoid “digital arrest”?","options":["Pay first and investigate later.","Keep the caller online while sharing an OTP.","Stop the interaction, preserve details, and contact the bank and official channels.","Forward the caller’s intimate recordings to friends."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The demand is a scam indicator. Preserve evidence and use independently verified reporting channels promptly.","unit":1}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
